---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Oscillating Tools",
    "url": "/best-oscillating-tool/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Jigsaws",
    "url": "/best-jigsaw/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Miter Saws",
    "url": "/best-miter-saw/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Angle Grinders",
    "url": "/best-angle-grinder/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Ryobi vs DeWalt",
    "url": "/ryobi-vs-dewalt/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Impact Drivers",
    "url": "/best-impact-driver/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-10-06", modified: "2026-10-06" });
const title = "6 Best Reciprocating Saws in 2026: Cordless, Corded, One-Handed";
const description =
  "Six reciprocating saws compared on stroke, SPM, size and battery platform, plus why most homeowners should start with a one-handed saw and which blade to use.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "milwaukee-2821-20",
    "name": "Milwaukee M18 FUEL SAWZALL Reciprocating Saw (2821-20)",
    "category": "Full-Size, 18V Brushless",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.7,
    "reviewCount": 1300,
    "pros": [
      "1-1/4\" stroke at up to 3,000 SPM, the numbers that set cutting speed",
      "About 17 inches long and roughly 6.8 lbs bare",
      "Brushless POWERSTATE motor that runs on any M18 battery",
      "Strong enough for demolition work that used to need a corded saw",
      "Around 1,300 Amazon ratings averaging 4.7"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Needs a large M18 pack to deliver its best speed",
      "No orbital setting for faster rough cuts in wood"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The cordless reciprocating saw that cuts like a corded one, and the one to buy if you do real demolition.",
    "description": "The 2821-20 is the saw most professional remodelers reach for when they need to take out a wall, cut through a nail-embedded stud or slice cast iron pipe, and the spec sheet explains why. It pairs a 1-1/4-inch stroke with up to 3,000 strokes per minute, which is the combination that removes the most material per second. Milwaukee calls it the fastest-cutting saw in its class; treat that as a marketing claim, but the stroke and speed figures back it up. It is roughly 17 inches long and about 6.8 lbs bare, so it is a full-size tool rather than a compact one, but it is well balanced and the brushless motor keeps speed under load better than older brushed saws. The practical limit is the battery. With a small 2.0 or 3.0Ah pack the saw is noticeably slower and will stop sooner on hard cuts. Pair it with a 6.0Ah pack or larger and it behaves like a corded saw. It does not offer orbital action, which some corded saws use to speed rough cuts in wood, but very few people miss it. If you are not on M18, start with the platform you already own."
  },
  {
    "id": "milwaukee-2719-20",
    "name": "Milwaukee M18 FUEL HACKZALL One-Handed Reciprocating Saw (2719-20)",
    "category": "One-Handed, 18V Brushless",
    "badge": "Best One-Handed",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.8,
    "reviewCount": 6500,
    "pros": [
      "About 4 lbs and 14 inches long, small enough to use with one hand",
      "7/8\" stroke, long for a one-handed saw",
      "Counterbalance mechanism Milwaukee says cuts vibration up to 4x",
      "Fits between joists, studs and under sinks where full-size saws cannot",
      "Over 6,500 Amazon ratings averaging 4.8"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Shorter stroke cuts more slowly than a full-size saw in thick wood",
      "Not the tool for large-scale demolition"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The reciprocating saw most homeowners should buy first: small enough to go anywhere, strong enough for pipe, branches and studs.",
    "description": "The Hackzall is the saw that made one-handed reciprocating saws a category. The FUEL version weighs about 4 lbs and is about 14 inches long, which means you can hold it in one hand at arm's length, under a sink, between floor joists or up in a tree while your other hand holds a branch or a ladder. Milwaukee uses a 7/8-inch stroke, long for a tool this size, and a dual-gear counterbalance mechanism that the company says reduces vibration by up to four times compared with traditional reciprocating saws. That matters more than it sounds: low vibration means faster cut starts in metal and a saw that does not wander off the line. In practice, the Hackzall handles plumbing pipe, conduit, nails and bolts, small branches, 2x lumber and drywall without strain. Where it gives ground is thick material and long cuts. The shorter stroke clears sawdust less effectively, so cutting through a 4x4 or a large root takes noticeably longer than with a full-size saw. For most homeowners that is a fair trade, because the majority of household cuts are small."
  },
  {
    "id": "makita-xrj05z",
    "name": "Makita 18V LXT Brushless Recipro Saw (XRJ05Z)",
    "category": "Full-Size, 18V Brushless, 2-Speed",
    "badge": "Best for Makita Owners",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.8,
    "reviewCount": 1100,
    "pros": [
      "1-1/4\" stroke with a two-speed setting: up to 2,300 or 3,000 SPM",
      "Low-speed range gives more control starting cuts in metal",
      "Makita lists 10\" wood capacity with a 12\" blade and 5-1/8\" pipe",
      "Electronically controlled brushless motor adjusts to the load",
      "About 1,100 Amazon ratings averaging 4.8"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Listed at about 8.2 lbs, among the heavier saws here",
      "Longer than the Milwaukee at roughly 17-1/4 inches"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A strong, controllable full-size saw for anyone already on Makita's 18V LXT batteries.",
    "description": "The XRJ05 is Makita's full-size 18V brushless reciprocating saw and a natural choice for anyone already invested in LXT batteries. It uses a 1-1/4-inch stroke and adds something the Milwaukee lacks: a two-speed selector, with a low range of up to 2,300 strokes per minute and a high range of up to 3,000. The low range is genuinely useful in metal, where a slower blade starts cuts more cleanly, runs cooler and lasts longer, and it gives better control in plaster and tile backer where a fast blade chatters. Makita lists a maximum cutting capacity of 10 inches in wood with a 12-inch blade and 5-1/8 inches in pipe, and the electronically controlled brushless motor adjusts speed and torque to the cut. It is a heavier tool than the Milwaukee: retailer listings put it at about 8.2 lbs, and it is about 17-1/4 inches long. In demolition, the weight helps the saw stay planted. Overhead, it adds up. If you own Makita batteries, there is no reason to switch platforms for a reciprocating saw. If you own none, decide which ecosystem suits the rest of your tool list first, because the saw is a small part of that decision."
  },
  {
    "id": "dewalt-dcs368b",
    "name": "DeWalt 20V MAX XR Brushless Reciprocating Saw with POWER DETECT (DCS368B)",
    "category": "Full-Size, 20V MAX Brushless",
    "badge": "Best for DeWalt Owners",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.7,
    "reviewCount": 170,
    "pros": [
      "1-1/8\" stroke at up to 3,000 SPM with a variable-speed trigger",
      "POWER DETECT: DeWalt claims up to 44% more power with an 8.0Ah pack",
      "Keyless lever-action blade clamp and an LED work light",
      "Comfortable, well-balanced handle for long demolition sessions",
      "Uses ordinary 20V MAX packs rather than requiring FLEXVOLT"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No orbital action, no rafter hook and no speed dial",
      "The power boost only appears with DeWalt's largest 20V packs"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The DeWalt 20V saw to buy, and the reason most DeWalt owners no longer need the 60V FLEXVOLT version.",
    "description": "DeWalt's DCS368 is the 20V MAX XR reciprocating saw with POWER DETECT, a system that recognizes DeWalt's high-capacity 8.0Ah battery and raises the tool's output when it is attached. DeWalt claims up to 44 percent more power in that configuration. With an ordinary 5.0Ah pack, it behaves like a strong conventional cordless saw. The base specifications are a 1-1/8-inch stroke and up to 3,000 strokes per minute through a variable-speed trigger, with a keyless lever-action blade clamp and an LED work light. Reviewers have praised the handle ergonomics and balance, and the saw has a reputation for smooth action and little kickback in heavy wood. What it lacks is equally clear. There is no orbital setting, no rafter hook and no speed dial, and reviewers note vibration control could be better than Milwaukee's. For most DeWalt owners, the DCS368 makes the 60V FLEXVOLT DCS389 unnecessary. That saw, also rated at 3,000 SPM with a 1-1/8-inch stroke, makes sense mainly if you already own FLEXVOLT batteries for other tools. The DCS368's Amazon rating count is still small, so treat its rating as provisional and lean on its specification and professional reviews."
  },
  {
    "id": "dewalt-dwe305",
    "name": "DeWalt 12 Amp Corded Reciprocating Saw (DWE305)",
    "category": "Full-Size, 12 Amp Corded",
    "badge": "Best Corded",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.7,
    "reviewCount": 3900,
    "pros": [
      "12 amp motor runs all day without batteries",
      "Up to 2,900 SPM with a 1-1/8\" stroke and variable-speed trigger",
      "Four-position blade clamp mounts the blade up, down, left or right",
      "Keyless lever-action blade changes",
      "Compact for a corded saw and long a best-seller"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Vibration control is basic compared with modern cordless saws",
      "Minimal feature set and a shoe that can block your view of the cut"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The cheapest way to get full-size reciprocating saw power if you do not want to buy into a battery platform.",
    "description": "The DWE305 is the corded reciprocating saw we would buy if you do not own a battery platform, or if the saw will be used for long, continuous demolition where batteries run out. It has a 12 amp motor, up to 2,900 strokes per minute through a variable-speed trigger and a 1-1/8-inch stroke, figures close to the best cordless saws. Its most useful feature is the four-position blade clamp: you can mount the blade teeth down, teeth up, or sideways, which makes flush cuts against a floor or wall far easier, and the clamp is keyless so changes take seconds. It has been one of the best-selling corded reciprocating saws for years, and professional reviewers have pointed out why: it is compact for a corded saw and the grip is comfortable on both the main and front handles. They also point out its weaknesses, which are vibration that is noticeably worse than in the best cordless saws, a shoe design that can block your view of the cut line and a very basic feature set with no orbital action. None of that matters much in demolition, which is what most people buy a corded saw for. We could not confirm a current Amazon rating count, so the figure shown is a conservative estimate."
  },
  {
    "id": "ryobi-pblrs01b",
    "name": "Ryobi ONE+ HP 18V Brushless Reciprocating Saw (PBLRS01B)",
    "category": "Full-Size, 18V Brushless",
    "badge": "Best Budget",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.5,
    "reviewCount": 140,
    "pros": [
      "1-1/4\" stroke at up to 3,200 SPM",
      "Brushless HP motor on the inexpensive ONE+ battery platform",
      "Pivoting shoe and tool-free blade release lever",
      "Ryobi claims over 380 cuts per charge",
      "Three-year manufacturer warranty"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Ryobi's speed claims come from its own testing, not comparisons with rivals",
      "Sold mainly through Home Depot, with a small Amazon review base"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The saw for ONE+ owners who need a full-size reciprocating saw for yard work and occasional demolition.",
    "description": "Ryobi's ONE+ HP brushless reciprocating saw is the value pick for people who already own Ryobi's 18V batteries, which is a very large number of homeowners. On paper it is competitive with saws that cost much more: Ryobi lists a 1-1/4-inch stroke and up to 3,200 strokes per minute, a brushless motor, a pivoting shoe and a tool-free blade release lever. Ryobi also claims 64 percent faster cutting and more than 380 cuts per charge, though those figures come from Ryobi's own testing, so read them as a sign that the HP version is a step up from older Ryobi saws rather than a comparison with Milwaukee or DeWalt. In practical terms, it is a capable saw for pruning, cutting up old fencing, removing a vanity or cutting through nails during a small renovation. It is less refined than the professional saws, with more vibration and a less durable build, and Ryobi tools are not designed for daily jobsite abuse. For homeowners, that line is rarely reached. It is sold mainly through Home Depot, so its Amazon rating count is small and the figure shown is conservative."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Reciprocating Saw",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-reciprocating-saw/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the difference between a Sawzall and a reciprocating saw?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Sawzall is Milwaukee's trademarked name for its reciprocating saws, the way Hackzall is its name for its one-handed saws. Every Sawzall is a reciprocating saw, but other brands like DeWalt, Makita and Ryobi make reciprocating saws too. People often use Sawzall to mean any reciprocating saw, much as they use Kleenex to mean any tissue."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a cordless reciprocating saw as powerful as a corded one?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The best brushless cordless saws now match a 12 amp corded saw in most cuts, provided they run on a large battery. Corded saws still win for long, continuous demolition because they never run down, and they cost less if you do not already own batteries. For most homeowners, cordless is more convenient and powerful enough."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a reciprocating saw cut metal?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, with the right blade. Use a bimetal blade with 14 to 24 teeth per inch for thin metal and 10 to 14 TPI for thicker steel and pipe. Keep at least three teeth in contact with the material, slow the saw down and use cutting oil on thick steel. Carbide-grit blades work best on cast iron."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a one-handed reciprocating saw worth it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most homeowners, yes. One-handed saws like the Milwaukee Hackzall weigh around 4 lbs and fit between studs, under sinks and in cabinets. They cut pipe, bolts, branches and lumber easily. They are slower than full-size saws in thick material, so demolition crews usually own both. Most households only need the smaller one."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use a reciprocating saw for tree trimming?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, for limbs you can reach safely from the ground. Fit a coarse pruning blade, keep the shoe against the branch and cut from the top unless the branch could pinch the blade. Do not use a two-handed saw on a ladder. For high branches, use a pole saw, and leave large limbs to a chainsaw or an arborist."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What does orbital action do on a reciprocating saw?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Orbital action moves the blade in a slight elliptical path so it digs in on the cutting stroke and lifts on the return. That speeds cuts in wood and clears sawdust better, but leaves a rougher cut and adds vibration. Always turn it off for metal. Many good cordless saws skip orbital action entirely."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long should a reciprocating saw blade be?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Choose a blade that reaches through the material plus an inch or two, so the tip clears the work on every stroke. Six-inch blades are stiffer and more accurate. Nine-inch blades are the general-purpose choice for demolition. Twelve-inch blades handle large limbs and thick timbers but flex more and are harder to control."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Tools & Equipment",
    tags: [
      "best reciprocating saw",
      "cordless reciprocating saw",
      "one-handed reciprocating saw",
      "sawzall vs hackzall",
      "reciprocating saw blades",
      "corded reciprocating saw"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            6 Saws Compared
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Reciprocating Saws</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          A reciprocating saw is the demolition tool that cuts wood, nails, pipe and branches. The right one depends on how often you take things apart and which batteries you already own.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#milwaukee-2821-20"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            See the Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="9.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#short-answer" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. The Short Answer</a>
          <a href="#specs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Stroke, SPM and Orbital</a>
          <a href="#types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Full-Size vs One-Handed</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Our 6 Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#choose" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. How We Would Choose</a>
          <a href="#cordless" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Cordless vs Corded</a>
          <a href="#blades" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Choosing Blades</a>
          <a href="#demo" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Demolition Technique</a>
          <a href="#safety" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Safety Gear</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most people who buy a reciprocating saw use it a few times a year: to cut out a rotten fence post, remove an old vanity, prune an overgrown shrub or cut through a stubborn bolt. For that work, a compact one-handed saw is easier to use than a full-size one. If you do renovation or demolition, a full-size brushless saw is worth every pound.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you are buying your first reciprocating saw and you do not do demolition for a living, buy a compact one-handed saw like the Milwaukee M18 FUEL Hackzall. It handles plumbing pipe, branches, nails, studs and drywall, and it fits places a full-size saw cannot. Most homeowners who buy a full-size saw first find it heavier and longer than they needed.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are taking out walls, cutting cast iron, pruning thick limbs or remodeling a whole room, buy a full-size saw. The Milwaukee 2821-20 is the best cordless one, and the Makita XRJ05Z and DeWalt DCS368B are the right choices if you already own those batteries. A corded saw like the DeWalt DWE305 is the cheapest way to get full-size power if you own no battery platform at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy within your battery platform. A reciprocating saw is one tool among many, and its battery is the expensive part. The differences between the Milwaukee, Makita and DeWalt saws on this list are real but small compared with the cost of starting a second battery system. Our <a href="/ryobi-vs-dewalt/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">Ryobi vs DeWalt comparison</a> is a good place to start if you have not chosen yet.
          </p>

          <p>
            A reciprocating saw is a demolition and rough-cutting tool. It is fast and it goes anywhere, but it is not accurate. If you need a clean curved cut in plywood, that is a <a href="/best-jigsaw/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">jigsaw</a> job, and if you need a precise plunge cut in a wall or a flush cut on trim, an <a href="/best-oscillating-tool/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">oscillating multi-tool</a> does it far better.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="specs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Stroke Length, SPM and Orbital Action
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two numbers determine how fast a reciprocating saw cuts. Stroke length is how far the blade travels on each pass, typically 7/8 inch on compact saws and 1-1/8 to 1-1/4 inches on full-size ones. Strokes per minute, or SPM, is how many passes it makes, usually around 3,000 at full trigger. Longer strokes clear more sawdust per pass and engage more teeth, so a 1-1/4-inch saw cuts thick wood noticeably faster than a 7/8-inch one.
          </p>

          <p>
            SPM matters more in metal than you might expect, but in the opposite direction. Faster is not better when cutting steel. A blade running at full speed in thick metal overheats, dulls quickly and chatters. That is why variable-speed triggers and two-speed selectors, like the one on the Makita XRJ05Z, matter. Slow down for metal and speed up for wood.
          </p>

          <p>
            Orbital action moves the blade in a slight elliptical path, so it pushes into the wood on the cutting stroke and lifts on the return. It cuts faster and clears sawdust better in wood, at the cost of a rougher cut and more vibration. It should always be switched off for metal. Several of the cordless saws here, including the DeWalt DCS368B, do without it, and most people never miss it.
          </p>

