          <p>
            Vibration is the spec nobody publishes and everyone feels. Counterbalance mechanisms, like the one Milwaukee uses in the Hackzall, cut the shaking that makes a saw wander off the line and tires your hands. A saw with poor vibration control is fine for a single afternoon of demolition and miserable for a week of it, so it is worth paying for if you use the saw often.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Full-Size, Compact and One-Handed Saws
          </h2>

          <p>
            Full-size reciprocating saws are 17 to 20 inches long, weigh 7 to 9 lbs with a battery and are held with two hands. They have the longest strokes and the most power, and they are the right tool for demolition, large-diameter pipe, thick limbs and anything where speed matters more than reach.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compact saws are a halfway house: shorter and lighter than full-size saws, but still two-handed. One-handed saws like the Hackzall go further, with a pistol grip and a total length around 14 inches. They give up stroke length and speed in exchange for the ability to cut between studs, inside cabinets, under sinks and overhead without fighting the tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most homeowners' cuts are small: a section of PVC drain, a rusted bolt, a branch the size of your wrist, a hole in a wall for a new box. One-handed saws do those cuts easily and are much more pleasant to use. The only time a homeowner really needs a full-size saw is during a major renovation, a big pruning job or when cutting cast iron pipe.
          </p>

          <p>
            For pruning, a reciprocating saw with a coarse pruning blade is fast for limbs you can reach from the ground. For anything higher, a <a href="/best-pole-saw/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">pole saw</a> is safer than a reciprocating saw on a ladder. Using a two-handed tool at the top of a ladder with one hand on the rung is how a lot of yard injuries begin.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Buy one-handed first unless you already know you need a full-size saw.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you find yourself wishing for more speed, a full-size saw is an easy second purchase. Most people who start with a full-size saw end up wanting a smaller one for everything else.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

