          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How We Would Choose Between Them
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the batteries on your shelf. If you own Milwaukee M18, the choice is between the Hackzall and the 2821-20, and many owners end up with both. If you own Makita LXT, the XRJ05Z is the obvious pick. DeWalt 20V owners should buy the DCS368B, and Ryobi ONE+ owners the PBLRS01B. Switching platforms for a reciprocating saw rarely makes sense.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, be honest about how often you will cut. Someone who removes a vanity once every few years and prunes a hedge every spring does not need a professional demolition saw. A one-handed saw or the Ryobi will do that work comfortably. Someone gutting a kitchen, replacing a bathroom floor or tearing out a deck should buy a full-size brushless saw and a large battery.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then think about where you will cut. Under sinks, inside cabinets, between studs and overhead, a short, light saw is easier to control and less tiring. In open demolition, on thick limbs and through cast iron, a longer stroke and more weight help. If you can only buy one saw and your work is mixed, the Hackzall covers more household jobs than a full-size saw does.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you own no batteries and expect occasional use, the corded DeWalt DWE305 is the cheapest way to get full-size performance, and it will still run in twenty years. Just remember an extension cord in demolition is a trip hazard and something the blade can reach, so route it behind you and away from the cut.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cordless" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Cordless vs Corded
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cordless reciprocating saws have caught up with corded ones for nearly all work. The best brushless saws on an 18V or 20V platform cut as fast as a 12 amp corded saw in most materials. The advantage of cordless is obvious in demolition, where you are moving around a room, climbing into crawlspaces and working where no outlet is nearby.
          </p>

          <p>
            Corded saws still have two advantages. They never run down, which matters on an all-day demolition job, and they cost less if you do not already own batteries. A corded saw also has no battery to fail after a few years. If you will use the saw once or twice a year and own no cordless platform, a corded saw is the sensible buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery size matters more on a reciprocating saw than on almost any other cordless tool. A small 2.0Ah pack will run a reciprocating saw, but slowly and briefly. A 5.0Ah or larger pack lets the saw reach its rated speed and keeps it there. Our <a href="/cordless-tool-batteries-explained/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">guide to cordless tool batteries</a> explains why amp-hours affect power as well as runtime.
          </p>

          <p>
            DeWalt also sells a 60V FLEXVOLT reciprocating saw, the DCS389, rated at 3,000 SPM with a 1-1/8-inch stroke. It is powerful, but the 20V DCS368 with POWER DETECT has closed much of the gap without requiring larger, heavier FLEXVOLT batteries. Unless you already own FLEXVOLT packs, the 20V saw is the more sensible choice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="blades" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Choosing the Right Blade
          </h2>

          <p>
            Blades matter as much as the saw. Reciprocating saw blades are sold by length, thickness and teeth per inch, or TPI. Low TPI blades, around 3 to 6, cut wood quickly and roughly and are the right choice for pruning and green wood. Mid-range blades, around 8 to 14 TPI, handle nail-embedded wood and general demolition. High TPI blades, 14 to 24, are for metal.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rule for metal is that at least three teeth should be in contact with the material at once. Thin-walled conduit and sheet metal need fine teeth, often 18 to 24 TPI. Thick steel and cast iron work better with 10 to 14 TPI bimetal blades, and cast iron especially benefits from carbide-grit or carbide-tipped blades, which last far longer than bimetal ones in that material.
          </p>

