          <p>
            Length matters too. A 6-inch blade is stiffer and more accurate, a 9-inch blade reaches through thicker material and a 12-inch blade cuts large limbs and timbers but flexes more. Choose the shortest blade that reaches through the material plus an inch or two, so the blade does not bottom out against the work on the forward stroke.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy a mixed pack of bimetal blades and a few dedicated pruning and metal blades, and treat them as consumables. A dull blade makes any saw feel underpowered, and the cheapest way to make a reciprocating saw cut faster is to change the blade sooner than you think you need to.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="demo" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Demolition Technique That Saves Time and Fingers
          </h2>

          <p>
            Press the shoe firmly against the work. The shoe is the metal foot at the front of the saw, and keeping it planted is the single most important technique with a reciprocating saw. With the shoe pressed against the surface, the saw cuts smoothly. With it floating, the blade bucks, the saw jumps and the blade can bind and snap.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start cuts at a lower speed, then squeeze the trigger fully once the blade is in the material. In metal, stay at a moderate speed throughout and use cutting oil on thick steel. For plunge cuts into drywall or wood, rest the shoe on the surface with the saw tilted, start the blade and slowly rotate the saw until the blade enters the material.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before cutting into any wall, floor or ceiling, find out what is behind it. Shut off the circuit, check for wiring and plumbing, and use a shallow blade where you can. A reciprocating saw will cut through electrical cable, water pipe and gas line just as easily as it cuts studs. If you are not sure, use an oscillating tool to cut a small inspection hole first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Expect the saw to kick if the blade binds. Keep both hands on a full-size saw and stand to the side of the blade line. Let the saw do the work. Forcing it overheats the motor and the blade, and on cordless saws drains the battery far faster than a steady, moderate pressure does.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Safety Gear and Habits
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wear eye protection every time. Reciprocating saws throw sawdust, metal chips and broken blade fragments toward your face, and demolition often happens overhead. Safety glasses with side shields are the minimum, and a face shield is worth wearing when cutting metal or overhead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hearing protection matters in demolition, where you may run a saw for hours. Gloves help with grip and protect against sharp metal edges, but avoid loose gloves near the blade. A dust mask or respirator is essential when cutting through plaster, old drywall or anything that may contain lead paint or other hazardous materials.
          </p>

          <p>
            Unplug corded saws and remove batteries from cordless ones before changing blades. Blades get very hot during cuts, especially in metal, so let them cool or use gloves. Store saws with the blade removed or covered, since a reciprocating saw blade sticking out of a tool bag is an easy way to get cut.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are cutting through bolts or other fasteners, an <a href="/best-impact-driver/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">impact driver</a> or impact wrench may remove them faster and more neatly than a saw, which saves blades and avoids the hot, sharp ends a cut leaves behind. For cutting heavy steel, rebar and tile, an <a href="/best-angle-grinder/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">angle grinder</a> with the right disc is often quicker.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting the shoe float off the work</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The shoe must stay pressed against the material during the cut. When it floats, the blade slaps back and forth, the saw jumps and blades bend or snap. Lean into the saw slightly so the shoe stays planted, and the cut will be faster, smoother and much less tiring for your hands and arms.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a wood blade on metal</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Coarse wood blades tear and snag in metal, chipping teeth and throwing the saw around. Metal needs fine-toothed bimetal blades, with at least three teeth in contact with the material at all times. Use cutting oil on thick steel and slow the saw down. A matching blade does more for speed than a bigger saw.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cutting into walls blind</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A reciprocating saw cuts through wiring, water lines and gas pipes as easily as studs. Shut off circuits, check for plumbing, and use a short blade or cut a small inspection hole first. Ten minutes of checking is cheaper than a burst pipe or a damaged circuit inside a finished wall.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying full-size for small jobs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A full-size saw is long, heavy and awkward under a sink or between joists. If most of your cuts are pipe, bolts and small branches, a one-handed saw does the job faster because you can actually get it into position. Buy a full-size saw when you know you need the extra stroke length.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a big saw on a tiny battery</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Reciprocating saws draw a lot of current, so a 2.0Ah pack makes even the best saw slow and short-lived. Use a 5.0Ah pack or larger for demolition and heavy cuts. If the saw feels weak, try a bigger battery before deciding the saw is the problem.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

