        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a Sawzall and a reciprocating saw?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sawzall is Milwaukee's trademarked name for its reciprocating saws, the way Hackzall is its name for its one-handed saws. Every Sawzall is a reciprocating saw, but other brands like DeWalt, Makita and Ryobi make reciprocating saws too. People often use Sawzall to mean any reciprocating saw, much as they use Kleenex to mean any tissue.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a cordless reciprocating saw as powerful as a corded one?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The best brushless cordless saws now match a 12 amp corded saw in most cuts, provided they run on a large battery. Corded saws still win for long, continuous demolition because they never run down, and they cost less if you do not already own batteries. For most homeowners, cordless is more convenient and powerful enough.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a reciprocating saw cut metal?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, with the right blade. Use a bimetal blade with 14 to 24 teeth per inch for thin metal and 10 to 14 TPI for thicker steel and pipe. Keep at least three teeth in contact with the material, slow the saw down and use cutting oil on thick steel. Carbide-grit blades work best on cast iron.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a one-handed reciprocating saw worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homeowners, yes. One-handed saws like the Milwaukee Hackzall weigh around 4 lbs and fit between studs, under sinks and in cabinets. They cut pipe, bolts, branches and lumber easily. They are slower than full-size saws in thick material, so demolition crews usually own both. Most households only need the smaller one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a reciprocating saw for tree trimming?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for limbs you can reach safely from the ground. Fit a coarse pruning blade, keep the shoe against the branch and cut from the top unless the branch could pinch the blade. Do not use a two-handed saw on a ladder. For high branches, use a pole saw, and leave large limbs to a chainsaw or an arborist.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does orbital action do on a reciprocating saw?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Orbital action moves the blade in a slight elliptical path so it digs in on the cutting stroke and lifts on the return. That speeds cuts in wood and clears sawdust better, but leaves a rougher cut and adds vibration. Always turn it off for metal. Many good cordless saws skip orbital action entirely.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should a reciprocating saw blade be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Choose a blade that reaches through the material plus an inch or two, so the tip clears the work on every stroke. Six-inch blades are stiffer and more accurate. Nine-inch blades are the general-purpose choice for demolition. Twelve-inch blades handle large limbs and thick timbers but flex more and are harder to control.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
